Presidential elections were held in [[Bulgaria]] on 6 November 2016,<ref>[https://www.ndi.org/electionscalendar Global elections calendar] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151022232515/https://www.ndi.org/electionscalendar |date=22 October 2015 }} NDI</ref> alongside [[2016 Bulgarian referendum|a referendum]] on changes to the electoral system and political party funding. The second round was held on 13 November 2016, resulting in the victory of [[Rumen Radev]].

==Electoral system==
The [[President of Bulgaria|president]] was elected using the [[two-round system]].<ref>[http://www.electionguide.org/elections/id/2207/ Bulgaria] IFES</ref> For the first time voters were allowed to vote for [[none of the above]].<ref>[http://sofiaglobe.com/2016/10/09/bulgarias-2016-presidential-elections-voters-to-have-i-dont-support-anyone-option/ Bulgaria’s 2016 presidential elections: Voters to have ‘I don’t support anyone’ option] Sofia Glove, 9 October 2016</ref>

==Candidates==
Incumbent president [[Rosen Plevneliev]] announced in May 2016 that he would not be running for re-election.<ref>{{cite web|title=Bulgarian President Rosen Plevneliev Won't Seek Second Term in 2016 Elections|url=http://www.novinite.com/articles/174558/Bulgarian+President+Rosen+Plevneliev+Won%E2%80%99t+Seek+Second+Term+in+2016+Elections|website=Novinite.com|agency=Sofia News Agency|access-date=27 May 2016}}</ref>

